module gunbc.runner.runner_host_surface

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import gunbc.build_cache_instance { ProcessIdentity }

// THE RUNTIME SURFACE IS DERIVED, NOT DISCOVERED. The invariant this module exists to make
// constructible: no user-space process, listener, route, executable, writable path, device grant or
// secret ingress exists on the runner host unless one modeled declaration here caused it. A red team
// should be able to enumerate the intended surface WITHOUT TOUCHING THE UNIT, because the same
// values that build and launch the machine are the ones that answer the question.
//
// A SCAN IS THE THIRD LAYER, NEVER THE FIRST. Three questions with three different authorities:
//
//   DECLARED   what processes and interfaces MAY exist          this module
//   ENFORCED   what prevents anything else existing             /init, namespaces, filter, image
//   OBSERVED   does THIS boot match the declaration exactly     whole-population runtime receipt
//
// A declaration alone is prose with better syntax. A scan alone describes whatever happened to
// start. The standing is the conjunction, and this module is deliberately only the first term.
//
// THE PROCESS IS THE ROOT, NOT THE PORT. A port is one projection of a running process, and a
// surface described as a port list cannot answer who owns the socket, what that owner may execute,
// what it may write, or what it may reach outbound. Modelling ports first is how a "surface
// document" becomes a fourth authority that happens to agree with sshd_config, the firewall and the
// probe -- which is four authorities, not one, and they agree only until they do not.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT YET CARRY, named so its absence is not read as a claim:
// capability sets, seccomp policy digests, mount grants, device grants and secret ingress are axes
// of RuntimeProcessClass in the ruling this follows, and are NOT modeled here. This slice models the
// process/listener/lineage spine and the exact-population judgment over it. An unmodeled axis is an
// axis nothing enforces, and the surface report must say so rather than imply completeness.

// CARDINALITY IS PART OF THE DECLARATION BECAUSE PIDS CANNOT BE. Nothing can name the pid a
// supervisor will get, so the declared population is a set of CLASSES with counts -- some fixed,
// some keyed by an admitted attempt, and one that is the whole point: Forbidden.
//
// ForbiddenClass IS NOT A COMMENT. It is the arm that makes "anything else" a representable,
// checkable state rather than the silent complement of a list.
type ProcessCardinality
  = ExactlyOne
  | ZeroOrMorePerControlSession
  | ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt
  | ForbiddenClass

// THE ROLES OF THE RAW RUNNER HOST, and the smallness is the design rather than an accident of this
// slice. In production the static user-space graph is a supervisor and a control ingress; everything
// else exists only because an attempt was admitted, and disappears when it retires.
//
// THERE IS NO IDLE FIRECRACKER. A hypervisor process with no admitted attempt behind it is not a
// warm pool, it is an unexplained process, and the population judgment treats it as one.
type HostProcessRole
  = HostSupervisor
  | ControlMaster
  | ControlSession
  | AttemptJailer
  | AttemptFirecracker

fn host_process_role_label(r: HostProcessRole) -> String {
  match r {
    HostSupervisor => "host-supervisor"
    ControlMaster => "control-master"
    ControlSession => "control-session"
    AttemptJailer => "attempt-jailer"
    AttemptFirecracker => "attempt-firecracker"
  }
}

// LINEAGE IS DECLARED, NOT INFERRED FROM WHATEVER RAN. "A firecracker exists" and "a firecracker
// exists BENEATH THE JAILER THAT WAS ADMITTED FOR THIS ATTEMPT" are different facts, and only the
// second is the isolation the jailer is there to provide. A firecracker parented anywhere else has
// escaped its jail by construction, whatever its command line says.
type ProcessParent
  = ParentIsKernel
  | ParentIsRole { role: HostProcessRole }

type HostProcessClass {
  role: HostProcessRole
  cardinality: ProcessCardinality
  parent: ProcessParent
  executable_digest: NonEmptyStr
  runs_as: NonEmptyStr
  may_spawn: List<HostProcessRole>
}

// ── the listener, at its real identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A LISTENER IS NOT A PORT NUMBER. These are four materially different surfaces and a model that
// records "22/tcp" cannot tell them apart:
//
//   127.0.0.1:22/tcp      reachable by nothing off-box
//   192.168.1.230:22/tcp  reachable from the management network
//   0.0.0.0:22/tcp        reachable from every attached network
//   [::]:22/tcp           the same mistake, in a family people forget to check
//
// The namespace matters for the same reason: an endpoint inside an attempt's network namespace is
// not on the host's surface at all, and conflating the two would report guest-side listeners as host
// exposure or, far worse, the reverse.
type AddressFamily = Inet4 | Inet6 | UnixDomain
type TransportProtocol = Tcp | Udp | UnixStream

type NetworkNamespaceScope
  = HostNetworkNamespace
  | AttemptNetworkNamespace

type ExposureZone
  = ManagementNetworkOnly
  | LoopbackOnly
  | HostLocalIpcOnly
  | AnyAttachedNetwork

type DeclaredListener {
  owner: HostProcessRole
  namespace_scope: NetworkNamespaceScope
  family: AddressFamily
  protocol: TransportProtocol
  local_address: NonEmptyStr
  port: Int
  zone: ExposureZone
}

// ── the declared surface of the raw runner host ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE PRODUCTION SURFACE. One supervisor, one control ingress, and nothing standing idle.
//
// THE CONTROL LISTENER IS A TRANSPORT TO ONE PROGRAM, NOT A SHELL. An ordinary SSH login re-opens
// the surface completely: it permits arbitrary combinations of every executable in the image, so the
// process population stops being closed even though the binary set is finite. Public-key only,
// forced command, no pty, no agent or TCP forwarding, management address only -- the caller reaches
// the host control agent and cannot reach anything else.
//
// THIS IS ALSO WHY THE FLEET'S ORDINARY SSH REALIZATION DOES NOT FIT HERE: it invokes `bash -s`,
// which both requires a shell this image should not contain and hands it arbitrary script text. The
// stateless host needs a typed control surface, not a generic exec transport.
data runner_host_production_processes: List<HostProcessClass> = [
  HostProcessClass {
    role: HostSupervisor,
    cardinality: ExactlyOne,
    parent: ParentIsKernel,
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [ControlMaster, AttemptJailer],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: ControlMaster,
    cardinality: ExactlyOne,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: HostSupervisor },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [ControlSession],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: ControlSession,
    cardinality: ZeroOrMorePerControlSession,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: ControlMaster },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "gunbc-control",
    may_spawn: [],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: AttemptJailer,
    cardinality: ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: HostSupervisor },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [AttemptFirecracker],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: AttemptFirecracker,
    cardinality: ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: AttemptJailer },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "attempt-scoped",
    may_spawn: [],
  },
]

// FIRECRACKER EXPOSES NO HOST LISTENER. Its API is either absent entirely, when the whole
// configuration is known before start, or an attempt-scoped Unix socket when lifecycle control needs
// it. Either way it is not a TCP endpoint on the host's surface, and a firecracker holding one is a
// finding rather than a configuration choice.
data runner_host_production_listeners: List<DeclaredListener> = [
  DeclaredListener {
    owner: ControlMaster,
    namespace_scope: HostNetworkNamespace,
    family: Inet4,
    protocol: Tcp,
    local_address: "management-address",
    port: 22,
    zone: ManagementNetworkOnly,
  },
]

// ── production and recovery are mutually exclusive ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// A RECOVERY SHELL IN THE IMAGE IS NOT A RECOVERY SHELL ON THE SURFACE. Carrying busybox so a
// console operator can diagnose a unit does not mean a network caller may reach it, and the two get
// conflated precisely because the binary is present either way. Modelling the boot PURPOSE keeps the
// distinction: a host in recovery refuses to admit VM work, and a host admitting VM work has no
// interactive shell reachable.
//
// THE DANGEROUS STATE IS THE UNION, and it is unrepresentable here rather than discouraged: there is
// no arm that is both.
type HostBootPurpose
  = RunnerHostProduction
  | RunnerHostRecovery

fn admits_vm_work(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => true
    RunnerHostRecovery => false
  }
}

fn admits_interactive_console_shell(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => false
    RunnerHostRecovery => true
  }
}

// ── the exact-population judgment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE EXPECTED POPULATION IS DERIVED FROM THE ADMITTED WORK, not fixed. Static singletons plus one
// jailer and one firecracker per admitted attempt plus the live control sessions -- so the same
// declaration answers "what should be running" at any moment without anyone maintaining a second
// list that drifts.
fn expected_process_count(admitted_attempts: Int, control_sessions: Int) -> Int {
  2 + control_sessions + (2 * admitted_attempts)
}

type ObservedProcess {
  identity: ProcessIdentity
  role_claimed: HostProcessRole
  parent_identity: ProcessIdentity
  executable_digest: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE FAILURES ARE TYPED AND EACH NAMES A DIFFERENT REMEDY. A single Bool here would be the
// typed-cause-erased failure this repository keeps finding: "the surface does not match" sends
// nobody anywhere, while "a firecracker is parented outside its jailer" and "sshd is listening on
// 0.0.0.0 instead of the management address" are different incidents with different owners.
//
// RuntimeSurfaceObservationUnreadable IS THE ARM THAT KEEPS THE OTHERS HONEST. An observation that
// could not be taken is not an observation of nothing, and folding it into "exact" would make a
// failed census the most reassuring outcome available.
type RuntimeSurfaceFinding
  = UnexpectedProcess { observed: ProcessIdentity, executable_digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | MissingExpectedProcess { role: HostProcessRole }
  | UnexpectedChildLineage { role: HostProcessRole, expected_parent: HostProcessRole }
  | ExecutableIdentityMismatch { role: HostProcessRole, expected: NonEmptyStr, observed: NonEmptyStr }
  | UnexpectedListener { local_address: NonEmptyStr, port: Int }
  | MissingExpectedListener { owner: HostProcessRole, port: Int }
  | ListenerOwnedByWrongProcess { port: Int, expected_owner: HostProcessRole }
  | ListenerInWrongNamespace { port: Int }
  | UnexpectedEgressRoute { destination: NonEmptyStr }
  | FirewallPolicyMismatch { detail: String }
  | RuntimeSurfaceObservationUnreadable { detail: String }

type RuntimeSurfaceStanding
  = RuntimeSurfaceHeld
  | RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: List<RuntimeSurfaceFinding> }

// COUNTS ARE NOT PROOF, and this function exists to say so in a place the compiler can see. Three
// expected and three observed is satisfied by three substitutions. The standing is an IDENTITY JOIN
// over roles, lineage and executable digests; the count is a cheap precondition that can only ever
// produce a finding, never discharge one.
fn runtime_surface_standing(findings: List<RuntimeSurfaceFinding>) -> RuntimeSurfaceStanding {
  if (findings |> count) == 0 {
    RuntimeSurfaceHeld
  } else {
    RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: findings }
  }
}

// A HOST THAT CANNOT ACCOUNT FOR ITSELF DOES NOT TAKE NEW WORK. An unexpected listener is not
// dashboard colour: it is an unexplained process holding a socket on a machine about to be handed
// someone else's code to run. Admission is gated on the standing rather than reported beside it.
fn admits_new_attempt(purpose: HostBootPurpose, standing: RuntimeSurfaceStanding) -> Bool {
  match standing {
    RuntimeSurfaceHeld => admits_vm_work(purpose: purpose)
    RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── the boundary of the claim ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THREE POPULATIONS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS CLAIMED EXACTLY. Stating this is what keeps the
// invariant honest rather than grandiose.
//
//   HOST USER-SPACE   exactly modeled, exactly observed -- the subject of this module
//   KERNEL SURFACE    config, built-in drivers, protocol families, syscall and LSM policy. Modeled
//                     separately because kernel threads are not ordinary processes and their
//                     population varies with hardware and activity.
//   GUEST WORKLOAD    deliberately arbitrary. An Actions job runs whatever it runs.
//
// WHAT IS CLOSED FOR THE GUEST IS ITS ENVELOPE, NOT ITS PROCESS LIST: no host namespace, no host
// mounts, no host devices beyond virtual ones, no inbound route, default-deny egress, one
// attempt-scoped credential, a dedicated kernel, bounded memory, cpu and disk. Those are the axes
// gunbc.product.fabric.isolation already names, and this module consumes that vocabulary rather than
// restating it.
type SurfacePopulation
  = HostUserSpaceExactlyModeled
  | KernelSurfaceModeledSeparately
  | GuestWorkloadArbitraryWithinEnvelope

// THE BMC IS OBSERVED AND MUST NEVER BE DERIVED. We do not build or launch its processes, so its
// surface cannot honestly come from our image -- it comes from an exact controller, an exact
// firmware identity, and an observed listener population. Presenting it beside the derived host
// surface as though both had the same provenance is the same portability error the MegaRAC lane is
// currently correcting: an observed property of one vendor build reported as a constructed property.
//
// So the asset report a red team receives is a COMPOSITION with its provenance visible per part,
// never one flat list.
type SurfaceProvenance
  = DerivedFromOurImage
  | ObservedFromVendorFirmware
  | ObservedFromNetworkFabric
